---
import AppShell from '../../layouts/AppShell.astro';
---

<AppShell mode="public"
  title="Bundles — LoopSkill Docs"
  description="What a bundle is, public vs private, personal vs Pro org-scoped, and pre-built skill bundles for client agents."
>
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-24">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Docs · Bundles</p>

    <!-- Phase C gating banner -->
    <div class="bg-yellow-500/10 border border-yellow-500/40 rounded p-4 mb-8">
      <p class="text-sm text-yellow-200">
        <strong>Coming Q2 2026 with per-bundle API keys and client deployment.</strong>
        The bundle creation UI and client deployment flow are shipping as part of Phase C.
        This page documents the intended feature so you can plan your setup.
        Everything described here is forward-looking and will be updated when Phase C ships.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight mb-6">
      Bundles
    </h1>
    <p class="text-muted text-lg leading-relaxed mb-12">
      A bundle is a named, versioned bundle of skills — like a playlist, but for your agent.
      Bundle the skills your team uses every day, share them across machines, and (on Pro)
      push them straight into clients' agents.
    </p>

    <!-- What a bundle is -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-4">What a bundle is</h2>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      A bundle is a manifest — a list of skill slugs at pinned versions — plus optional
      metadata (name, description, visibility). When an agent syncs against a bundle, it
      installs exactly the skills listed, at exactly the versions pinned, and nothing else.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted mb-10">
      You create and manage bundles from the <a href="/library" class="text-accent hover:opacity-80 underline">/library</a>
      dashboard. Free accounts get 1 bundle. Pro accounts get unlimited bundles.
    </p>

    <!-- Public vs Private -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-4">Public vs private bundles</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border rounded-xl p-5">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text mb-2">Public</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">Anyone with the bundle URL can install it. Useful for sharing a curated skill set with the community or documenting your team's standard toolkit publicly.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-bg-card border border-border rounded-xl p-5">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text mb-2">Private</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">Accessible only via an API key you explicitly share. Clients or teammates install with your key — the bundle content is never publicly discoverable.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Personal vs org-scoped -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-4">Personal vs org-scoped (Pro)</h2>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      <strong class="text-text">Personal bundles</strong> belong to your account and sync to
      the machines where you run agents with your API key.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted mb-10">
      <strong class="text-text">Org-scoped bundles</strong> (Pro) belong to your Pro account
      and can be deployed to agents you operate for clients. Each deployment gets its own
      bundle-scoped API key so the client's agent can only access the skills you chose —
      nothing else in your private catalog.
    </p>

    <!-- Pre-built catalogs for clients -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-4">Pre-built catalogs for clients (Pro)</h2>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      The canonical use case for Pro bundles: you operate AI agents for clients. Instead of
      manually installing skills on each client's machine, you:
    </p>
    <ol class="flex flex-col gap-3 mb-10 text-sm text-muted list-none">
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="flex-none w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-accent/10 text-accent text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center">1</span>
        <span>Build a bundle in your library — pick the skills, pin the versions.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="flex-none w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-accent/10 text-accent text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center">2</span>
        <span>Assign a dedicated API key scoped to that bundle.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="flex-none w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-accent/10 text-accent text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center">3</span>
        <span>Give your client the one-line meta-skill install command that includes that key.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="flex-none w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-accent/10 text-accent text-xs font-bold flex items-center justify-center">4</span>
        <span>The client's agent installs the meta-skill, the bundle auto-syncs, and stays up to date on every poll cycle.</span>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="text-muted mb-10">
      When you push an update to the bundle — adding a skill, bumping a version — all agents
      that installed via that key receive the update on their next sync. One push, every client.
    </p>

    <!-- Whitelabel -->
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mb-4">White-label guidance</h2>
    <p class="text-muted mb-10">
      The client-side experience never mentions LoopSkill branding unless you choose to include it.
      The install command, the skills, the skill output — all run locally on the client's machine.
      No LoopSkill UI, no LoopSkill domain in their agent's context. You bill them under your own
      brand at your own rate.
    </p>

    <div class="p-6 bg-bg-card border border-border rounded-2xl flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-start sm:items-center justify-between gap-4">
      <div>
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text">Ready to deploy bundles to clients?</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">See the deployment walkthrough for a full step-by-step guide.</p>
      </div>
      <a href="/docs/deployment" class="shrink-0 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-4 py-2 rounded-lg text-sm font-semibold border border-border hover:border-accent text-text transition">
        Deployment guide →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</AppShell>
